ion  overhaul is this the way out of this  crisis not necessarily because what we  have seen here is that a newly  neoliberal policy economic policy which  has kind of structurally disadvantaged a  massive portion of the population unless  and until we address that just having a  constitutional change doesn't go very  far let's say for example two different  sectors here one is education and the  other one is health care so if the  country doesn't invest in that if the  country doesn't open up those two  sectors to the middle class  the rising on the class so to speak then  the constitutional reform is not going  to go very far do you see any chances of  that happening these sectors being  opened up no what has happened here is  that exactly what we have in Mexico  because the rich grow richer and the  grow poorer and that is exactly what is  happening in Chile it is a success story  yes but it is a success story only for  only a specific section of the society  if you look at the OECD figures Chile  sits at the bottom in terms of economic  disparity the gap that we talked about  earlier so unless and until we address  the specific structural readjustment  then the discontent is going to continue  and whatever kind of window dressing we  are going to do so this constitutional  reform is not going to address the real  questions at the ground level  a melinda meza from Lancaster University  thank you for your thoughts
 

